G. Full Model Derivations of the Unconstrained Model
In the unconstrained model, primary wage hours \(h^{w,1} \in [\underline{h},\, T]\) are optimized jointly with secondary hours. The SE program is identical to the constrained model (no primary wage job to adjust), so I refer to Appendix Appendix F for the SE FOCs, concavity, and comparative statics. I present here the derivations for W, WW, and WSE that differ from the constrained case, and for the propositions that rely on the unconstrained specification.

Wage employment (\(W\)): FOC and closed form
The worker solves \[V_i^W = \max_{h^{w,1} \in [\underline{h},\, T]} \left\{
\frac{(w_1\,h^{w,1})^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}
- \chi\,\frac{(h^{w,1})^{1+\eta_{w_1}}}{1+\eta_{w_1}}
\right\}.\] The interior FOC is \[w_1^{1-\sigma}\,(h^{w,1})^{-\sigma} = \chi\,(h^{w,1})^{\eta_{w_1}},
\tag{FOC-W}\] which solves to \[h^{*,W} = \left(\frac{w_1^{1-\sigma}}{\chi}\right)^{1/(\eta_{w_1}+\sigma)}.\] The second derivative is \(\Phi''(h) = -\sigma w_1^2(w_1 h)^{-\sigma-1} - \chi\,\eta_{w_1}\,h^{\eta_{w_1}-1} &lt; 0\), so the objective is strictly concave and the optimum is unique. This closed form is also what the calibration of \(\chi\) inverts at the median wage-only worker (Section 9.3).


Dual wage employment (\(WW\)): FOC and Hessian

Program and FOCs
The dual jobholder jointly optimizes primary and secondary wage hours: \[V_i^{WW} = \max_{h^{w,1},\, h^{w,2}} \left\{
\frac{(w_1 h^{w,1} + w_2 h^{w,2})^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}
- \chi\,\frac{(h^{w,1})^{1+\eta_{w_1}}}{1+\eta_{w_1}}
- \chi\,\frac{(h^{w,2})^{1+\eta_{w_2}}}{1+\eta_{w_2}}
\right\} - \gamma_{MH},\] subject to \(\underline{h} \leq h^{w,1}\), \(\underline{h} \leq h^{w,2}\), and \(h^{w,1}+h^{w,2} \leq T\). The additive \(\gamma_{MH}\) sits outside the maximisation. Denoting \(c = w_1 h^{w,1} + w_2 h^{w,2}\), the interior FOCs are \[\begin{aligned}
w_1\,c^{-\sigma} &= \chi\,(h^{w,1})^{\eta_{w_1}}, \\
w_2\,c^{-\sigma} &= \chi\,(h^{w,2})^{\eta_{w_2}},
\end{aligned}
\tag{FOC-WW}\] and a time-boundary corner with \(h^{w,1}+h^{w,2} = T\) is solved when the interior solution is infeasible.


Negative definiteness of the Hessian
The Hessian with respect to \((h^{w,1}, h^{w,2})\) is \[H = \begin{pmatrix}
-(a+b) & -f \\
-f & -(d+e)
\end{pmatrix},\] where \[a = \sigma w_1^2 c^{-\sigma-1},\quad
b = \chi\,\eta_{w_1}(h^{w,1})^{\eta_{w_1}-1},\quad
d = \sigma w_2^2 c^{-\sigma-1},\quad
e = \chi\,\eta_{w_2}(h^{w,2})^{\eta_{w_2}-1},\] and \(f = \sigma w_1 w_2\,c^{-\sigma-1}\). All five quantities are strictly positive. The off-diagonal term satisfies \(f^2 = a\cdot d\) exactly (since both are products of \(\sigma^2 w_1^2 w_2^2 c^{-2\sigma-2}\)). Hence \[|H| = (a+b)(d+e) - f^2 = (a+b)(d+e) - ad = ae + bd + be &gt; 0.\] Since \(H_{11} &lt; 0\) and \(|H| &gt; 0\), the Hessian is negative definite by Sylvester’s criterion. The optimum is unique.



Hybrid entrepreneurship (\(WSE\)): FOC and Hessian

Program and FOCs
The worker jointly optimizes primary wage hours and entrepreneurial hours: \[\begin{aligned}
\tilde{V}_i^{WSE} = \max_{h^{w,1},\, h^{se}} \biggl\{
\mathbb{E}_{\tilde{y}}\!\left[\frac{\bigl(w_1 h^{w,1}+(1+\tau)\rho^{m}\,\tilde{y}\,h^{se}\bigr)^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}\right]
&- \chi\gamma\phi\,\frac{(h^{se}+S_L)^{1+\eta_{se}}}{1+\eta_{se}}\\
&- \chi\,\frac{(h^{w,1})^{1+\eta_{w_1}}}{1+\eta_{w_1}}
\biggr\} - \gamma_{MH},
\end{aligned}\] subject to \(\underline{h} \leq h^{w,1} \leq T\) and \(0 &lt; h^{se} \leq T - h^{w,1}\), with the additive \(\gamma_{MH}\) outside the maximisation. I focus on the micro interval (\(\mathbb{E}[\tilde{y}]\,h^{se} \leq M\)). Denoting \(c = w_1 h^{w,1}+(1+\tau)\rho^{m}\,\tilde{y}\,h^{se}\), the interior FOCs are \[\begin{aligned}
w_1\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[c^{-\sigma}\right] &= \chi\,(h^{w,1})^{\eta_{w_1}}, \\
\mathbb{E}\!\left[\tilde{y}(1+\tau)\rho^{m}\,c^{-\sigma}\right] &= \chi\gamma\phi\,(h^{se}+S_L)^{\eta_{se}}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{FOC-WSE}\] The standard entrepreneur FOCs are obtained by replacing \((1+\tau)\rho^{m}\) with \(\rho^{r}\) and \(S_L\) with \(S_H\).


Negative definiteness of the Hessian
The Hessian with respect to \((h^{w,1}, h^{se})\) is \[H = \begin{pmatrix}
-(a+b) & -f \\
-f & -(d+e)
\end{pmatrix},\] where now \[\begin{gathered}
a = \sigma w_1^2\,\mathbb{E}[c^{-\sigma-1}],\qquad
b = \chi\,\eta_{w_1}(h^{w,1})^{\eta_{w_1}-1},\\
d = \sigma(1+\tau)^2(\rho^{m})^2\,\mathbb{E}[\tilde{y}^2\,c^{-\sigma-1}],\qquad
e = \chi\gamma\phi\,\eta_{se}\,(h^{se}+S_L)^{\eta_{se}-1},
\end{gathered}\] and \(f = \sigma w_1(1+\tau)\rho^{m}\,\mathbb{E}[\tilde{y}\,c^{-\sigma-1}]\). Unlike the WW case, \(f^2 \neq ad\) in general. By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality applied to the expectations: \[\begin{aligned}
f^2 = \sigma^2 w_1^2(1+\tau)^2(\rho^{m})^2\,\bigl(\mathbb{E}[\tilde{y}\,c^{-\sigma-1}]\bigr)^2
&\leq \sigma^2 w_1^2(1+\tau)^2(\rho^{m})^2\,\mathbb{E}[c^{-\sigma-1}]\,\mathbb{E}[\tilde{y}^2\,c^{-\sigma-1}]\\
&= a\cdot d.
\end{aligned}\] Hence \(f^2 \leq ad\) and the same calculation gives \(|H| = (a+b)(d+e) - f^2 \geq ae + bd + be &gt; 0\). Since \(H_{11} &lt; 0\) and \(|H| &gt; 0\), the Hessian is negative definite and the optimum is unique.



Derivations for Proposition 6: cross-wage substitution in \(WW\)
The two FOCs define \((h^{w,1}, h^{w,2})\) implicitly as functions of parameters. Totally differentiating with respect to a parameter \(p\) and writing in matrix form: \[H \begin{pmatrix} dh^{w,1}/dp \\ dh^{w,2}/dp \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} -F^1_p \\ -F^2_p \end{pmatrix},\] where \(F^i_p \equiv \partial F^i/\partial p\) is the direct effect of \(p\) on the \(i\)-th FOC (holding hours fixed). By Cramer’s rule: \[\begin{aligned}
\frac{dh^{w,1}}{dp} &= \frac{-H_{22}\,F^1_p + H_{12}\,F^2_p}{|H|}, \qquad\text{(IFT-1)} \\
\frac{dh^{w,2}}{dp} &= \frac{H_{21}\,F^1_p - H_{11}\,F^2_p}{|H|}. \qquad\text{(IFT-2)}
\end{aligned}\]

Own-wage response \(\partial h^{w,2}/\partial w_2\).
The direct effects are \(F^1_{w_2} = -\sigma w_1 h^{w,2}\,c^{-\sigma-1}\) and \(F^2_{w_2} = c^{-\sigma}(1-\sigma s_2)\), where \(s_2 = w_2 h^{w,2}/c\) is the second-job income share. Substituting into (IFT-2) and simplifying (the \(ad\) terms cancel using \(f^2 = ad\)): \[\frac{\partial h^{w,2}}{\partial w_2}
= \frac{c^{-\sigma}[a + b(1-\sigma s_2)]}{|H|}.\] For \(\sigma &lt; 1\): since \(s_2 &lt; 1\), the factor \((1-\sigma s_2) &gt; 0\) pointwise, so the numerator is strictly positive and \(\partial h^{w,2}/\partial w_2 &gt; 0\). The own-wage response of the first job, \(\partial h^{w,1}/\partial w_1\), follows by the same argument, mutatis mutandis, with \(s_1 = w_1 h^{w,1}/c\) and curvature \(\eta_{w_1}\) in place of \(\eta_{w_2}\): \[\frac{\partial h^{w,1}}{\partial w_1}
= \frac{c^{-\sigma}[d + e(1-\sigma s_1)]}{|H|} &gt; 0 \quad (\sigma &lt; 1).\]


Cross-wage response \(\partial h^{w,1}/\partial w_2\).
Substituting into (IFT-1), expanding, and using the FOC \(w_2\,c^{-\sigma} = \chi(h^{w,2})^{\eta_{w_2}}\) to simplify: \[\frac{\partial h^{w,1}}{\partial w_2}
= \frac{-\sigma w_1\,c^{-\sigma-1}\,\chi(1+\eta_{w_2})(h^{w,2})^{\eta_{w_2}}}{|H|} &lt; 0 \quad (\text{all } \sigma &gt; 0).\] The key step is to substitute \(w_2\,c^{-\sigma} = \chi(h^{w,2})^{\eta_{w_2}}\) from the second FOC into the \(f\cdot c^{-\sigma}(1-\sigma s_2)\) term of the numerator. After expanding, the \((1-\sigma s_2)\) piece and the \(e\cdot\) term combine into an expression proportional to \(\chi(1+\eta_{w_2})(h^{w,2})^{\eta_{w_2}}\), leaving only products of positive quantities with an overall minus sign. By symmetry: \[\frac{\partial h^{w,2}}{\partial w_1}
= \frac{-\sigma w_2\,c^{-\sigma-1}\,\chi(1+\eta_{w_1})(h^{w,1})^{\eta_{w_1}}}{|H|} &lt; 0 \quad (\text{all } \sigma &gt; 0).\] Cross-wage effects are unambiguously negative: the two wage jobs act as substitutes at the intensive margin. This is a pure income effect, as a higher wage on one job raises total consumption without making the other job more productive.



Derivations for Proposition 7: reallocation channel in \(WSE\)
The two WSE FOCs define \((h^{w,1}, h^{se})\) as functions of parameters. I apply the same IFT approach as above: \[\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial h^{*,1}_{wse}}{\partial p} &= \frac{-H_{22}\,G^1_p + H_{12}\,G^2_p}{|H|}, \qquad\text{(IFT-1')} \\
\frac{\partial h^*_{wse}}{\partial p} &= \frac{H_{21}\,G^1_p - H_{11}\,G^2_p}{|H|}. \qquad\text{(IFT-2')}
\end{aligned}\]

Response to \(\tau\).
The direct effects on the two FOCs are: \[\begin{aligned}
G^1_\tau &= -\sigma w_1\rho^{m}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\tilde{y}\,h^{se}\,c^{-\sigma-1}\right] &lt; 0, \\
G^2_\tau &= \rho^{m}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\tilde{y}\,c^{-\sigma}\bigl(1-\sigma\,s_e(\tilde{y})\bigr)\right],
\end{aligned}\] where \(s_e(\tilde{y}) = (1+\tau)\rho^{m}\,\tilde{y}\,h^{se}/c \in (0,1)\) is the realization-specific entrepreneurial income share. For \(\sigma &lt; 1\): \(\sigma\,s_e &lt; 1\) pointwise, so \(G^2_\tau &gt; 0\). The response to \(\rho^{m}\) has the same structure with \((1+\tau)\) in place of \(\rho^{m}\) in front of each expectation.
Substituting into (IFT-1\('\)): the first term is \(-H_{22}\,G^1_\tau = -(-(d+e))(-) &lt; 0\). The second term is \(H_{12}\,G^2_\tau = (-)(+) &lt; 0\). Both terms are negative, so \[\frac{\partial h^{*,1}_{wse}}{\partial\tau} &lt; 0 \quad (\sigma &lt; 1).\] Substituting into (IFT-2\('\)): the first term is \(H_{21}\,G^1_\tau = (-)(-) &gt; 0\). The second term is \(-H_{11}\,G^2_\tau = -(-(a+b))(+) &gt; 0\). Both terms are positive, so \[\frac{\partial h^*_{wse}}{\partial\tau} &gt; 0 \quad (\sigma &lt; 1).\] A more favorable micro regime induces the hybrid entrepreneur to shift hours from wage work to entrepreneurship.


Response to \(\mu_y\).
Using \(\partial\tilde{y}/\partial\mu_y = \tilde{y}/\mu_y\): \[G^1_{\mu_y} = \frac{-\sigma w_1(1+\tau)\rho^{m}}{\mu_y}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\tilde{y}\,h^{se}\,c^{-\sigma-1}\right] &lt; 0,
\qquad
G^2_{\mu_y} = \frac{(1+\tau)\rho^{m}}{\mu_y}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\tilde{y}\,c^{-\sigma}\bigl(1-\sigma\,s_e(\tilde{y})\bigr)\right].\] For \(\sigma &lt; 1\), \(G^2_{\mu_y} &gt; 0\) by the same pointwise argument. The sign analysis is identical to the \(\tau\) case: \[\frac{\partial h^{*,1}_{wse}}{\partial\mu_y} &lt; 0, \qquad
\frac{\partial h^*_{wse}}{\partial\mu_y} &gt; 0 \quad (\sigma &lt; 1).\]


Response to \(\phi\).
Only the second FOC is affected: \(G^1_\phi = 0\) and \(G^2_\phi = -\chi\gamma(h^{se}+S_L)^{\eta_{se}} &lt; 0\). Substituting into (IFT-1\('\)) and (IFT-2\('\)): \[\frac{\partial h^{*,1}_{wse}}{\partial\phi}
= \frac{H_{12}\,G^2_\phi}{|H|}
= \frac{(-)(-)}{(+)} &gt; 0,
\qquad
\frac{\partial h^*_{wse}}{\partial\phi}
= \frac{-H_{11}\,G^2_\phi}{|H|}
= \frac{(+)(-)}{(+)} &lt; 0.\] A weaker taste for variety (\(\phi\) closer to 1) reduces entrepreneurial hours and increases primary wage hours: the worker reallocates time back toward the wage job. These reallocation effects are mechanically absent in the constrained model where \(\partial\bar{h}/\partial p = 0\) for all \(p\).
For \(\sigma &gt; 1\) and sufficiently large draws of \(\tilde{y}\), \(G^2_\tau\) and \(G^2_{\mu_y}\) become ambiguous (the income effect can dominate on the entrepreneurial side), and the signs of the reallocation effects can reverse. As in Proposition 3, the threshold for ambiguity is strictly above one: at \(\sigma = 1\) exactly, \(\sigma\,s_e &lt; 1\) holds pointwise since \(s_e &lt; 1\) always.
